        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Zionism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Founded among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>’s Pale of Settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>; its settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, under Ottoman and then British rule, and from 1948 the State of Israel; support and lobbying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the Jewish diaspora.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Forerunners and the Lovers of Zion (1860s–1890s; first settlement 1882); the First Zionist Congress and the Basel Program (1897); the Second Aliyah (from 1904); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a> (1917) and the British Mandate (1922–1948); the Biltmore Program (1942); the 1948 war and the State of Israel; since then Israel’s dominant ideology.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a>, author of Der Judenstaat and convener of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-zionist-congress">First Zionist Congress</a>; Leon Pinsker, author of Auto-Emancipation; Ahad Ha’am, founder of cultural Zionism; Chaim Weizmann, head of the World Zionist Organization; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a>, Labor Zionist leader who declared the State of Israel; Ze’ev Jabotinsky, founder of Revisionist Zionism; Abraham Isaac Kook, religious Zionist thinker.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The State of Israel, declared on 14 May 1948, which by the end of the war controlled over 78% of former Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>; the 1948 Palestinian expulsion and flight, in which an estimated 710,000 Palestinians were driven out; the immigration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the Arab world; a lasting conflict with the Palestinians and a sharp debate over the movement’s nature.</span></div>
        </div>
