    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Zionism arose in the late 19th century among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, in reaction to new waves of antisemitism and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haskalah">Jewish Enlightenment</a>, holding that Jews are a nation with a right to self-determination.</li><li>Through settlement, land purchase, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a> of 1917 and the British Mandate, the movement built the institutions of a Jewish quasi-state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a> declared the State of Israel in 1948.</li><li>Its advocates see a national liberation movement returning an indigenous people to its ancestral homeland; Palestinians and other opponents see a colonial or settler-colonial movement, pointing to the expulsion and flight of Palestinians in 1948, which Palestinians call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakba">Nakba</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word “Zionism” was first used by the Austrian writer Nathan Birnbaum in an article of 1890. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a>, unaware of this, popularized the word himself, and did not call himself a Zionist until months after publishing Der Judenstaat in 1896.</p></aside>
    </section>

